                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Table 4.1: Appearance, hardness, and effect of hammering on different objects or materials"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 2,
              "bbox": [
                0.118,
                0.133,
                0.848,
                0.441
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_2_table_2.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tr><td>S.No.</td><td>Object/Material</td><td>Appearance(lustrous/non-lustrous)</td><td>Hard/Soft</td><td>Effect of hammering(flattens/breaks intopieces)</td></tr><tr><td>1.</td><td>Piece of copper</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2.</td><td>Piece of aluminium</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td>Iron nail</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>4.</td><td>Piece of coal</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5.</td><td>Lump of sulfur(pea-sized)</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6.</td><td>Block of wood</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.458,
            0.744,
            0.587
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Analyse",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Table 4.1. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Identify",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the objects that are "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "lustrous",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in appearance and "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "hard",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". You might have observed that objects made from copper, aluminium, and iron are lustrous in appearance and are hard. Lustre shown by metals is known as metallic lustre. Materials like copper, aluminium, and iron are known as "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "metals",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", whereas, coal, sulfur, and wood are "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "non-lustrous",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and not as hard as metals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.588,
            0.744,
            0.7
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Are all metals hard and solid? Not really; some metals like sodium and potassium are so "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "soft",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " that they can be cut with a knife. There is one metal, mercury, that is found in a liquid state at room temperature, which you might have seen in thermometers. Recall the chapter ‘Temperature and its Measurement’ from the Grade 6 Science textbook Curiosity."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.701,
            0.743,
            0.739
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Which objects did you find become flat on beating with a hammer?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.74,
            0.745,
            0.909
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You must have observed that objects such as a piece of copper, an iron nail, and a piece of aluminium become flat when beaten; whereas other objects or materials behave differently. This property by which materials can be beaten into thin sheets is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "malleability",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Most metals possess this property. Can you give some examples of metal sheets? You might have seen thin silver foil on some sweets and aluminium foil used for wrapping food items. These are formed due to their malleability. Gold and silver are the most malleable metals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "aside_text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.958,
            0.119,
            0.981,
            0.362
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The World of Metals and Non-metals"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 8,
